A||||6||WisconsinGT||Permatex Ultra Copper
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||08-08-99||12:43 AM||||Has anyone used it? What are your thoughts? I want to use it instead of normal gaskets on my exhaust manafolds. For that matter, will it work for all other gasket areas? 
Z||000001||PaUL||08-08-99||01:31 AM||||Ive used that silicone before. It doesnt hold up when exposed to exhaust manifold heat, especially if your manifolds are like mine and dont fit flush against the head. I just tried using permatex muffler and exhaust sealer instead of gaskets. It works but I still get a slight leak right after first starting. As the exhaust gets hotter, the leak goes away. The RTV works fine in other areas and is more resistant to heat than any other silicone Ive tried. 
Z||000002||theogre||08-08-99||11:52 AM||||no type of RTV/Silicon sealer I know of is designed for exhaust work. If the flanges are screwed up, there isn't much that will help except truing the flanges or replacing the manifold.
Z||000003||lowCG||08-08-99||12:44 PM||||The heat,and even the pressure,if not excessive isn't the problem with using silicone on exhausts,it's the combustion by-products that break down the compound.<br>The type of gasket needed on this motor,and I don't know if anyone makes them,is a thin,brass one with a ridge formed to seal around the ports.
Z||000004||theogre||08-08-99||01:01 PM||||someone here tried some simple brass rings and had good luck... tacked them with RTV if I remember.<p>Make sure any silicon you use is "sensor safe." Other wise you'll kill the O2 sensor.
Z||000005||PaUL||08-08-99||08:17 PM||||yeah Ogre, its was me. I used the brass rings on the trunk side manifold and it worked really great. The bulkhead side however was a different story. I couldnt align the rings with the ports because I couldnt see. Ive been trying different things, the last idea I had was to use 1/8 inch thick aluminum as gaskets. I got them in an old power supply I had. They were used as heat sinks. I got this idea when I saw this new type of gasket made out of "dead aluminum" whatever that is. I figure the aluminum is soft enough to conform to the irregularities of the sealing surfaces. 
Z||000006||lowCG||08-08-99||10:37 PM||||I think the aluminum would work fine,made a spacer to move the header out,and away from the starter on my dad's big block truck once,used gaskets though.I used some tool-grade stuff,don't know if what you're using has the same properties though.<br>I think I'll try to see if some Alfa Romeo gaskets can be made to fit,they look the same,and have worked well since the fifties on every 4cyl.car they produced.Might try the ones from an early 105 series(1300cc-1600cc).
